# Kenwood TS-890S PC CONTROL COMMAND REFERENCE GUIDE
# Printed revision/date: no revision printed; cover date "January/30/2019".
# Command: EX "Set or Read the Menu"
# Printed page 24 = PDF page 25. Charts used: Set, Read, Answer.
# Derivation date: 08/09/2026.
# NO code, no generator, no other file and no other document was consulted.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COUNTED FRAME LENGTHS
#   Read       : 8 positions. Ruler 1..10; cells 'E' 'X' P1 P2 P2 P3 P3 ';'.
#                Terminator at printed position 8 - a NUMBER.
#   Set/Answer : 8 fixed positions, then P5, then ';'.
#                The ruler prints, tier by tier:
#                  tier 1 header  1  2  3  4  5  6  7  8  9 ~
#                         cells   E  X P1 P2 P2 P3 P3 P4  P5
#                  tier 2 header  x
#                         cells   ;
#                The ninth column of tier 1 is headed "9 ~" and the terminator's position
#                header is the LETTER "x", not a number. See crop work/ex890-chart.png.
#                Frame length = 8 + len(P5) + 1; terminator position x = 9 + len(P5).
#
# POSITION MAP (Set and Answer), with each legend VERBATIM
#   1      'E'
#   2      'X'
#   3      P1 (Menu type number)
#            "0:  Menu"
#            "1:  Advanced Menu"
#   4- 5   P2 (Category number)
#            "00 ~ 99"
#            "Entering a non-existing number causes an error to occur."
#            "Enter any value when using the Advanced Menu."
#   6- 7   P3 (Item number)
#            "00 ~ 99"
#            "Entering a non-existing number causes an error to occur."
#            "Entering a number that cannot be set also causes an error to occur."
#   8      P4 (Configuration classification)
#            "Space:  Normal Configuration"
#            "9:  Initial value setting (setting command only)"
#            "Response is always a space."
#            [The value for Normal Configuration is a real ASCII space byte in the frame.]
#   9~     P5 (String of alphanumeric characters for the Menu setting)   -- VARIABLE LENGTH
#            "Normally a 3-digit number (blank digits must be entered as "0".)"
#            "PF key settings use 4 digits (refer to the PF Key assignment ID lists)."
#            "A power-on message can vary in length from 0 to 15 characters."
#            "Screen saver text can vary in length from 0 to 10 characters."
#            "Refer to the Menu tables below for the EX Command Parameter lists."
#            "Entering a value other than the listed values causes an error to occur.)"
#   x      ';'   -- printed position header is the LETTER x
#
#   Read chart carries P1, P2, P3 only; P4 and P5 are absent and ';' sits at position 8.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INHERITED-ASSUMED bytes
#   * P2 and P3 (category 00 / item 00) and the P5 value "000": arbitrary in-range choices.
#     The book's own EX Command Parameter Lists (which start after this page) were not read
#     for this witness, so no specific menu item is claimed. Legal ranges 00~99 / 00~99 are
#     the book's.
#   * P4 in the Set vector is the ASCII space byte for "Normal Configuration"; the space is
#     manual-determined, its position (8) is counted.
#   * ex_set_initialize below uses P4 = '9'. The book does NOT say whether P5 is still sent
#     when P4 is '9' ("Initial value setting (setting command only)"). ASSUMED that P5 is
#     still present and carries the same width as an ordinary setting.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VECTORS  (name <TAB> frame bytes verbatim; the byte at position 8 of the Set/Answer
#           vectors is a real ASCII space)
read	EX00000;
set_normal	EX00000 000;
answer_normal	EX00000 000;
set_initialize	EX000009000;
